  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I confirmed each one by reading it back.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 30 fields, including `timestamp` (date), `Carrier` (string), `Cancelled` (boolean), `DistanceKilometers` (number), and `AvgTicketPrice` (number). The time field is `timestamp`.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-bbbdee85 Flights' (id `s4-bbbdee85-flights-35b1e4fa081c`) has two panels:\n   - A line chart of flight count over `timestamp` (date histogram).\n   - A bar chart of flight count by `Carrier` (top 10 terms).\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-bbbdee85 Cancelled flights' (id `57fe65f2-78de-4306-8217-5f5ca34bfe3d`) is an `.es-query` rule with these settings:\n   - Runs every 1m on `kibana_sample_data_flights`.\n   - Counts documents where `Cancelled` is true over a 5-minute window on `timestamp`.\n   - Fires when the count is greater than 10.\n\n   The rule was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n\n4. **Panel rename:** The carrier panel is now titled 'Flights by carrier'. The dashboard read-back shows the new title. I then read the rule back, and it reports `enabled: true` with status `ok`.\n\nI chose a line chart for the over-time panel and a bar chart for the carrier panel. Say if you want different chart types.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
